Migrations for the application's own tables.

The schema is declared in `app/db/schema.py` as SQLAlchemy Core metadata, and
that is the single source of truth — these revisions describe how to get an
existing database to it, not what it is.

    alembic current                     # where this database stands
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"
    alembic upgrade head                # startup does this automatically

Two things that are not obvious:

* **`sde_*` is not here.** CCP's static data is dropped and rebuilt wholesale on
  every SDE build, so putting it under migration control would mean a revision
  every time CCP adds a column to something we do not own. It is created
  directly by `apply_sde_schema()`; `include_object` in `app/db/migrate.py`
  keeps it out of autogenerate.

* **The URL comes from the application.** `alembic.ini` deliberately leaves
  `sqlalchemy.url` unset; `env.py` takes it from `app.db.location.database_url()`,
  which reads `EVE_DATABASE_URL` and otherwise points at the local SQLite file.
  That environment variable is the seam the Postgres cutover goes through.

If `test_the_migrations_match_the_declaration` fails, someone changed
`app/db/schema.py` without generating a revision. Generate it.
